                <!-- <h1>Software Development Models (or Methodologies)</h1> -->
                <p><strong>Software development models (or methodologies)</strong> are structured approaches for planning, managing, and executing the software development process. They help teams organize tasks, reduce risks, and deliver quality products efficiently. Here are some of the most widely used models:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li>Waterfall Model</li>
                    <li>Agile Model</li>
                    <li>Spiral Model</li>
                    <li>V-Model (Verification and Validation)</li>
                    <li>DevOps Model</li>
                </ol> 
                <ol>
                    <h2><li>Waterfall Model</li></h2>
                    <p>The Waterfall model is a linear and sequential approach. Each phase must be completed before moving to the next.</p>
                    <p>Phases:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Requirements gathering</li>
                        <li>System design</li>
                        <li>Implementation</li>
                        <li>Testing</li>
                        <li>Deployment</li>
                        <li>Maintenance</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Pros:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Simple and easy to understand</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Works well for small projects with clear requirements</p>
                    <p>Cons:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Inflexible to changes</li>
                        <li>Issues are often discovered late</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2><li>Agile Model</li></h2>
                        <p>The <strong>Agile model</strong> is an iterative and flexible approach that focuses on continuous improvement and customer feedback.</p>
                        <!-- <p>Key principles:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Individuals and interactions over processes and tools</li>
                        </ul> -->
                        <p>Popular Agile frameworks include:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Scrum</li>
                            <li>Kanban</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Key features:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Short development cycles (sprints)</li>
                            <li>Frequent releases</li>
                            <li>Close collaboration with stakeholders</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Pros:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Highly adaptable</li>
                            <li>Faster delivery of usable features</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Cons:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Requires strong team coordination</li>
                            <li>Less predictable timelines</li>
                        </ul>

                    <h2><li>Spiral Model</li></h2>
                        <p>The <strong>Spiral model</strong> combines elements of both design and prototyping with a focus on risk analysis.</p>
                        <p>Steps in each cycle:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Planning</li>
                            <li>Risk analysis</li>
                            <li>Engineering</li>
                            <li>Evaluation</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Pros:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Good for large, complex, and high-risk projects</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Cons:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Expensive and complex</li>
                            <li>Requires expertise in risk assessment</li>
                        </ul>

                    <h2><li>V-Model (Verification and Validation)</li></h2>
                    <p>The <strong>V-Model</strong> is an extension of the Waterfall model, where testing is planned alongside development.</p>
                        <p>Key idea:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Each development phase has a corresponding testing phase</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Pros:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Early testing improves quality</li>
                            <li>Clear structure</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Cons:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Still rigid like Waterfall</li>
                            <li>Not suitable for changing requirements</li>
                        </ul>

                    <h2><li>DevOps Model</li></h2>
                        <p><strong>DevOps</strong> is a modern approach that integrates development and operations teams to improve collaboration and automation.</p>
                        <p>Key practices:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Continuous Integration (CI)</li>
                            <li>Continuous Delivery (CD)</li>
                            <li>Automation of testing and deployment</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Pros:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Faster and more reliable releases</li>
                            <li>Better collaboration</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Cons:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Requires cultural and technical changes</li>
                            <li>Initial setup can be complex</li>
                        </ul>
                </ol>
                <h2>Conclusion</h2>
                <p>Each software development model has its strengths and is suited for different types of projects. For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Use Waterfall for stable, well-defined projects</li>
                    <li>Use Agile for dynamic and evolving requirements</li>
                    <li>Use Spiral for high-risk systems</li>
                    <li>Use DevOps for continuous changing and faster delivery</li>
                </ul>
                <p>In practice, many teams combine elements from multiple models to create a hybrid approach that best fits their needs.</p>
            `
